{
  "name": "gridrunner",
  "description": "Gridrunner (Jeff Minter, HES, 1982), the 4K C64 cartridge, loaded at $8000-$8FFF.\n\nA CBM80 autostart cartridge: $8000-$8008 is a vector table, not code, and execution begins at ColdStart $83C1. $8E00-$8FFF is a 64-character font copied to $2000 before anything else runs; the whole game is drawn with those characters and there are no sprites.\n\nShape of the program:\n  ColdStart $83C1 -> CopyCharsetToRam $82EC -> InitVicSidAndScreen $8100 -> DrawStatusLine $8806 -> TitleScreen $8D8E\n  TitleScreen: joystick up picks a level 1..31, fire starts -> ResetScoreAndLives $80E5 -> NewLevel $8C2D\n  NewLevel -> LevelStartFanfare $8D16 -> StartLevel $8300 -> MainLoop $8393\n  MainLoop: UpdatePlayer, PauseOnKeyP, nine updates at $83A3, a short delay, forever\n  PlayerDies $8ADE resets the stack -> PlayerDeathExplosion $8AF8 -> LoseALife $8C17 -> RestartSameLevel, or CheckHighScore $8060 -> TitleScreen\n  WaveClearedCheck $8AC8 is the only other exit from the loop: it jumps to NewLevel\n\nTiming. The game installs no interrupt handler of its own - $0314 and $D01A are never written - but it does not run with interrupts off either: ColdStart calls RESTOR and then CLI, so the ordinary 60Hz KERNAL IRQ runs underneath, which is what keeps $C5 current for the P-key pause. The game never waits for it. Everything is paced by counting main-loop iterations against two counters, tick $0D (period 128) and phase $0F (period 24), with each subsystem testing them at a different VALUE so the work spreads across iterations.\n\nNo randomness. Exactly two instructions read the I/O page, both LDA $DC11 for the joystick. No raster line, no SID oscillator, no CIA timer, no jiffy clock. Given a starting level and a sequence of joystick inputs the game is fully deterministic.\n\nThe screen is the model. Collision is done by reading the character already in the cell being moved into; a droid s damage state is the character code on screen and nothing else; score, high score and lives are digits in screen memory at $040F, $041B and $0427, with no binary copy anywhere. Everything drawn goes through PlotCharAndColour $8172, which has 35 callers.\n\nDroids move as chains: droidFlags bit6 marks a head and bit7 a tail, a follower copies the position of the entry one place ahead, and shooting the middle of a chain splits it into two chains ($8AA4) rather than shortening it.\n\nOf 4096 bytes none are unexplained. About 110 are unreachable: NOP padding, four little routines with no callers, one byte of level-table data the level cap can never index, and - the interesting one - the tail of the charset-copy loop stranded at $8009 when the CBM80 header was placed over its first nine bytes.",
