        { "id": "lbl_bxpqd2", "address": "$8D70", "name": "LevelIntroDone", "type": "code" },
        { "id": "lbl_gk4nwt", "address": "$8D78", "name": "ErasePlayerAndExplode", "type": "code" },
        { "id": "lbl_vw763r", "address": "$8D8E", "name": "TitleScreen", "type": "code" },
        { "id": "lbl_bl6cx5", "address": "$8D94", "name": "PrintTitleStrings", "type": "code" },
        { "id": "lbl_n9gelg", "address": "$8DB0", "name": "TitleWaitForInput", "type": "code" },
        { "id": "lbl_7kogy3", "address": "$8DC0", "name": "TitleShowLevel", "type": "code" },
        { "id": "lbl_uxjt2m", "address": "$8DC6", "name": "TitleStartGame", "type": "code" },
        { "id": "lbl_lt5a06", "address": "$8DE0", "name": "textByJeffMinter", "extent": 16 },
        { "id": "lbl_e26koz", "address": "$8DF0", "name": "textEnterLevel", "extent": 16 },
        { "id": "lbl_ncmzen", "address": "$8E00", "name": "charsetData", "extent": 512 },
        { "id": "lbl_x3zjca", "address": "$8E38", "name": "glyphPlayerShip", "extent": 8 },
        { "id": "lbl_s16fqb", "address": "$8E58", "name": "glyphUnusedMirrorPair", "extent": 16 },
        { "id": "lbl_82tsl3", "address": "$8E98", "name": "glyphSegmentAndBlast", "extent": 24 },
        { "id": "lbl_5dg98x", "address": "$8ED8", "name": "glyphLetterYAndItsDuplicate", "extent": 16 },
        { "id": "lbl_0w4k0s", "address": "$8EF8", "name": "glyphUnusedUpArrow", "extent": 8 }
      ],
      "comments": [
        { "id": "cmt_v5shm2", "address": "$8100", "text": "One-time hardware and screen init, entered from CopyCharsetToRam and never called.\n\nIt writes the VIC through a zero-page pointer rather than absolutely: $02/$03 are set to $D000 and then STA ($02),Y with Y=$18, $20, $21 writes $D018 (screen $0400, charset $2000) and clears the border and background to black. A range search over $D000-$D02E finds none of these three writes - they are the only VIC register writes in the whole program, and there are no sprites at all.\n\nThen the SID envelopes, then the row-address table: for each of 24 rows it stores the low byte of the row base in screenRowLo and the high byte in screenRowHi and fills the row with $20. That table is what makes PlotCharAndColour a handful of instructions instead of a multiply." },
        { "id": "cmt_hskoml", "address": "$8163", "text": "Turn (plotCol, plotRow) into a screen pointer: screenPtr = screenRowLo/Hi[plotRow], Y = plotCol. The whole game addresses the screen this way." },
        { "id": "cmt_7kdtff", "address": "$8172", "text": "PLOT. Writes plotChar to the screen and plotColour to colour RAM at (plotCol, plotRow). 35 callers - every single thing this game draws goes through here, because there are no sprites.\n\nThe colour address is derived by adding $D4 to the high byte of the screen pointer: $04xx + $D400 = $D8xx. That works only because the screen is at $0400 and colour RAM at $D800, and it is why moving the screen would break the game.\n\n$8180-$8185 is three dead instructions - LDA $07 / LDA $07 / STA $07 - six wasted bytes that load a value and store it back to where it came from. Almost certainly the remains of an edit." },
        { "id": "cmt_1mnsv7", "address": "$818B", "text": "Read the character currently at (plotCol, plotRow). This is the game's entire collision system: instead of comparing coordinates, it asks the screen what is already in the cell it is about to move into. Nine callers, and every one of them is a collision test." },
        { "id": "cmt_qjojtn", "address": "$8191", "text": "Retrigger voices 1 and 2: CTRL=$00 (gate off) then CTRL=$81.\n$81 = %10000001 = noise waveform + gate on. Both voices are noise, which\nis the whole of Gridrunners explosion/zap palette - there is no music." },
        { "id": "cmt_sgnl28", "address": "$82EC", "text": "Copy the 512-byte font from $8E00-$8FFF to $2000-$21FF, where $D018 will point the VIC at it. Two 256-byte copies in one loop with X starting at 0, so the first iteration handles offset 0 and the loop runs the full 256 - the standard DEX/BNE-from-zero idiom. Nothing in the game ever reads $8E00 again." },
        { "id": "cmt_i7k404", "address": "$8E00", "text": "THE CHARACTER SET: 64 glyphs, 8 bytes each, copied to $2000 by\nCopyCharsetToRam. It is a CUSTOM font, so no built-in PETSCII or\nscreen-code table reads it. Mapping, read off the bitmaps and confirmed\nby decoding four strings:\n  $00-$1F  game objects and two small labels - see the note at $2000\n  $20 space\n  $21 G  $22 R  $23 U  $24 I  $25 D  $26 N  $27 E  $28 A  $29 B\n  $2A F  $2B H  $2C J  $2D M  $2E P  $2F S\n  $30 O and 0 (one glyph serves both)   $31-$39 digits 1-9\n  $3A T  $3B V  $3C $3D the two halves of a (c)  $3E L  $3F |\nThe letters C K Q W X Z do not exist. The alphabet is packed in the order\nMinter needed it, not A-Z. Confirmed by decoding $8080 to\n\"(c) 1982 HES  PRESS FIRE TO BEGIN\", $8DE1 to \"BY JEFF MINTER\", $8DF1 to\n\"ENTER LEVEL 00\", $8C51 to \" BATTLE  STATIONS \" and $8C62 to\n\"ENTER GRID AREA 00\".\n\nFOUR OF THE 64 GLYPHS ARE DEAD. $0B and $0C are an exact mirrored pair\n(every byte bit-reversed) that nothing loads and no table contains;\n$1C is byte-identical to $1B (the letter Y) and only $1B is used; $1F is\nan up arrow nothing draws. That is 6% of the font left over.\nEstablished with find_immediates on each value plus a scan of all five\ncharacter tables ($8080, $8820, $8C51, $8DE1, $871F).\n\nNOTE FOR A READER: I put per-glyph comments and labels on $8E38, $8E58,\n$8E98, $8ED8 and $8EF8. They are stored and list_comments and list_labels\nwill show them, but a bitmap region renders neither, so they do not appear\nbelow. That is a gap in the tool, not a mistake in the data." },
        { "id": "cmt_8smpui", "address": "$8080", "text": "Title-screen copyright string, 32 bytes, in the games custom character\nset - NOT PETSCII and NOT C64 screen codes. Reads:\n  \"(c) 1982 HES  PRESS FIRE TO BEGIN\"\nIndexed 1-based from $807F, so $8080 is byte 1." },
        { "id": "cmt_c1iuhg", "address": "$80A0", "text": "Draw the two-digit level number on the title screen. level ($35) wraps\nback to 1 at $20, so the selectable range is 1-31. The digits are counted\nup rather than divided: increment the units cell, and when it passes $39\n(9) reset it to $30 (0) and carry into the tens cell. They are written\nstraight into screen RAM at $0557/$0558 = row 8, columns 23 and 24, which\nis exactly where \"ENTER LEVEL 00\" left its two 0s." },
        { "id": "cmt_r7kv1v", "address": "$80D2", "text": "Draw the title-screen copyright line. X counts $20 down to 1 over\nLDA $807F,X, so the 32 bytes $8080-$809F land at screen $0593-$05B2 =\nrow 10, columns 3-34, all in colour $07 (yellow).\nThrough the games own character set the text reads:\n  \"(c) 1982 HES  PRESS FIRE TO BEGIN\"\n($3C and $3D are the left and right halves of the (c) symbol.)" },
        { "id": "cmt_9qb3c2", "address": "$8D8E", "text": "TITLE SCREEN. Sets the level to 1, prints \"BY JEFF MINTER\" and \"ENTER LEVEL 00\" and the copyright line, then loops in TitleWaitForInput.\n\nThe level select is genuinely a level select: joystick up increments `level`, fire starts the game there. It is read at $8DB0 by comparing the raw $DC11 byte against $EF (fire) and $FE (up), rather than through ReadJoystick - a second, independent piece of joystick code with the opposite polarity convention." },
        { "id": "cmt_48w0e3", "address": "$8DB0", "text": "Title-screen input, and it is a SECOND, independent piece of joystick code. It compares the raw $DC11 byte against $EF (fire, bit4 low) and $FE (up, bit0 low) - active low, where ReadJoystick $8373 inverts to active high. Two conventions in one program because the title screen predates or postdates the game loop; nothing shares." },
        { "id": "cmt_8qksj9", "address": "$8DE1", "text": "Two 14-byte title strings in the games custom character set:\n  $8DE1 \"BY JEFF MINTER\"\n  $8DF1 \"ENTER LEVEL 00\"\nEach is indexed 1-based from the $EA byte before it. $8DFF (a space) is\nnever read. Decode with the projects \"Gridrunner custom font text\"\ndecoder; every built-in encoding renders these as nonsense." },
        { "id": "cmt_z4bkkn", "address": "$8000", "text": "Nine bytes of cartridge header. The KERNAL's reset code looks for the five bytes C3 C2 CD 38 30 (\"CBM80\" with the top bit set on the first three) at $8004 and, finding them, jumps through $8000 instead of finishing its own startup - so this cartridge takes control before BASIC exists. $8000 = $83C1 (cold start), $8002 = $83E2 (NMI). Declaring this as code, which is what the project did before anyone looked at it, disassembles a vector table." },
        { "id": "cmt_4wizod", "address": "$8020", "text": "Look at the cell at (plotCol, plotRow); if it holds the ship glyph $07, the player is dead. Used by the horizontal zapper beam. The routine returns the character it read, so the caller can also use it as a plain read - which FireZapperBeam does at $868D." },
        { "id": "cmt_forv3g", "address": "$8060", "text": "GAME OVER: compare the seven score digits against the seven hi-score digits most-significant first. The comparison is on character codes, which works because $30-$39 are contiguous and in order. First digit that differs decides it: BMI means the score is lower, so no new record. Equal all the way through also means no record. Then back to the title screen. Nothing is saved anywhere - it is a cartridge, and the hi-score lives only in screen memory at $041B." },
        { "id": "cmt_csmz7o", "address": "$8300", "text": "START LEVEL. Draws the grid with DrawGridWipe, plays the materialise effect, then sets the per-level state: player at column $14 row $15 (bottom centre), no laser ($11=$FF), zappers at their starting positions, all 24 bomb slots freed, droidCount 0. Ends by jumping into the main loop and never returns.\n\n$834E-$8357 is five dead instructions: LDA $2B / STA $2B / STA $2B / LDA $2A / STA $2A. They read a value and write it back. NewLevel has already loaded both from the level tables, so this looks like the remains of code that used to compute them here." },
        { "id": "cmt_zdncse", "address": "$8373", "text": "Read joystick port 1 and invert it into `joystick`. The address is $DC11, which is not a documented register - CIA1's sixteen registers repeat every 16 bytes through $DC00-$DCFF, so $DC11 is $DC01, port B. EOR #$FF turns the active-low switches into active-high bits: bit0 up, bit1 down, bit2 left, bit3 right, bit4 fire." },
        { "id": "cmt_zjqly8", "address": "$8393", "text": "THE MAIN LOOP. Three addresses form one cycle: MainLoopTop $83A0 jumps here; here calls UpdatePlayer and PauseOnKeyP and jumps to MainLoopUpdates $83A3, which calls nine update routines and jumps to MainLoopDelay $83E8, which burns 21 iterations of DEX and jumps back to MainLoopTop.\n\nThere is no interrupt and no raster sync anywhere in the program. The NMI vector is a bare RTI, no IRQ vector is ever installed ($0314 is never written), and $D01A is never touched. The loop free-runs, so the game's speed is whatever this code happens to take, and every subsystem is paced by counting loop iterations rather than frames.\n\nTwo counters do the pacing, and the trick is that different subsystems test them at DIFFERENT values, so the work spreads out instead of all landing on one iteration:\n\n  tick $0D - UpdatePlayer decrements it. ReloadTickIfWrapped $89A0 puts it back to $80 the moment it reads $FF, so the cycle is 128 iterations long.\n    == 0  UpdatePlayer moves the player\n    == 1  UpdateZappers runs (and then halves itself again with zapperStepDelay)\n    odd   ExplosionSfxStep runs\n    == $FF ReloadTickIfWrapped resets it\n\n  phase $0F - UpdateLaser decrements it and reloads it to $18, so 24 iterations.\n    == 0  UpdateLaser steps the shot\n    == 5  FireZapperBeam fires, and NextBeamChar $86D0 decrements phase a SECOND time while a beam is running, which is how the beam gets several iterations out of one slot.\n\nSo `CMP #$05 / BEQ` at the top of a routine is not a magic number, it is a time slot, and phase is decremented from two places." },
        { "id": "cmt_nhwbip", "address": "$83A3", "text": "The nine per-iteration updates, in order: laser, zappers, zapper beam, droid animation, bombs, explosion sound, droids, tick reload, wave-cleared check. Each one begins by testing its own counter and returning immediately if this is not its slot, so most iterations run almost nothing." },
        { "id": "cmt_tqqj31", "address": "$83C1", "text": "COLD START. The KERNAL reset routine jumps here through the $8000 cartridge vector, before it has finished its own initialisation, so this routine has to do the rest by hand: IOINIT ($FDA3), RAMTAS ($FD50), RESTOR ($FD15) and CINT ($E518). SCROLX=$05 first, which is 38-column mode with a horizontal scroll of 5. LDA #$08 / JSR CHROUT sends the control code that LOCKS the character set, so nothing can flip to lower case and destroy the game's own font. Then straight into CopyCharsetToRam; it never returns and there is no RTS." },
        { "id": "cmt_a9rk2k", "address": "$83E2", "text": "The $8002 half of the cartridge vector, which the KERNAL uses as the NMI entry - i.e. RESTORE. It pulls the three registers the NMI handler pushed and RTIs, so pressing RESTORE does nothing at all. That is the point: it makes the game unbreakable from the keyboard. It is also why the block analysis reports this routine with a stack delta of -3 rather than the ordinary -2." },
        { "id": "cmt_m4w1tp", "address": "$83E8", "text": "The whole of the game's frame pacing: 21 iterations of DEX/BNE. That is roughly 100 microseconds, so it does not pace anything by itself - it exists to stop the loop being quite as tight as it otherwise would be. Note $83F0-$83FF is unreachable NOP filler with a stray JMP MainLoopTop at $83FA that nothing jumps to." },
        { "id": "cmt_rj6bm2", "address": "$8470", "text": "UPDATE PLAYER, and the sole reader of the joystick. Runs when tick reaches 0 - which is once per 128 main-loop iterations, because ReloadTickIfWrapped puts tick back to $80 as soon as it wraps to $FF, not per 256.\n\nOrder matters and is worth reading carefully:\n1. Look at the cell the player is standing in. If it does not still hold the ship glyph, something has been drawn over the player since last time; DieUnlessHarmlessChar decides whether that is fatal.\n2. Erase the old position to an empty grid cell.\n3. Apply the joystick to plotCol/plotRow. Columns clamp to 1..$26, rows to $0F..$15 - seven rows at the bottom of the grid, which is the band the player is confined to. The grid itself runs rows 2..$15, so the player can reach only the bottom third of it.\n4. Look at the cell being moved INTO. If it is not empty, PlayerRanIntoChar.\n5. Save the new position to playerCol/playerRow and draw the ship.\n\nThe clamping is written as \"if it went one past, put it back\", not as a test before moving, so plotRow/plotCol briefly hold an illegal coordinate inside this routine. Nothing reads them in between, but a reader tracing the bounds should not expect to find a comparison.\n\nNote what is NOT here: there is no test of the fire button. Firing is UpdateLaser's business, on a different counter." },
        { "id": "cmt_jdx1za", "address": "$84F8", "text": "UPDATE LASER - the player's shot, and there is exactly one slot for it in the whole program. laserRow = $FF means nothing is in flight, and the fire button is only read when it is $FF, which is why pressing fire does nothing while a shot is on screen.\n\nA shot is launched from the player's cell one row up (laserCol/laserRow from playerCol/playerRow, then DEC), with laserChar $08 and laserSfx $40.\n\nEach step: look at the cell ahead. Anything that is neither empty nor the shot's own current glyph goes to LaserHitsChar. Then draw the shot and INC laserChar; the glyph alternates $08 and $09, and when the increment reaches $0A the shot moves up one row and laserChar goes back to $08. So the two glyphs are one cell's worth of animation and the shot advances a row every second step.\n\nReaching row 2 - the top of the grid - retires the shot by setting laserRow back to $FF. So does a hit: both exits of LaserHitsChar write $FF there before discarding their return address.\n\nIt also decrements `phase`, which is the counter FireZapperBeam is gated on, so the laser is the clock for the zapper beams as well as for itself." },
        { "id": "cmt_na0ttm", "address": "$859B", "text": "UPDATE ZAPPERS - the two edge sweepers. One walks down column 0 (leftZapperRow, rows 3..$15, glyph $01), the other walks along row $16 (bottomZapperCol, columns 1..$26, glyph $02). Each step erases with $20 and redraws.\n\nWhen zapperFireCountdown runs out it is reloaded from zapperFireInterval - a per-level value - and a beam is armed: beamActive=$FF, and the two zappers' current positions are frozen into beamRow/beamVertCol for FireZapperBeam to use. That is the mechanic: the beams fire from wherever the sweepers happen to be, so the danger is predictable if you watch them." },
        { "id": "cmt_bhhkja", "address": "$8635", "text": "FIRE ZAPPER BEAM, and it does more than kill: it is where new droids come from once a level is under way.\n\nRuns only when phase is 5 AND beamActive is $FF (armed by UpdateZappers). NextBeamChar advances beamChar and also decrements phase again, which is how the beam keeps its own slot for several iterations. beamChar cycles $05/$06, wrapping at $07 - the CMP #$07 at $8646 is that wrap, NOT a test for the ship glyph, which is what it looks like at a glance and what I bound a constant to before reading it properly.\n\nEach pass:\n1. Redraw the vertical beam in white down column beamVertCol from row $15 up to row 3.\n2. At (beamCol, beamRow), read the cell. If it already holds beamChar the horizontal sweep has caught up with itself and the beam is over.\n3. Otherwise erase that cell, advance beamCol, and look at the new one with CharIsPlayerThenDie - which is where the beam kills.\n4. Draw the leading edge. Note $869A: CLC then SBC #$01, so the glyph is beamChar MINUS TWO, not minus one.\n\nEraseZapperBeam $86A2 then clears the vertical line back to grid cells - and drops a droid glyph $0F, in yellow, at (beamVertCol, beamRow): the intersection of the two beams. plotCol is still the vertical beam column at that point, which is easy to misread as a leftover. So the two edge zappers firing across each other is not just a hazard, it seeds a new droid where they cross, and AnimateDroidsOnScreen picks that glyph up on its next scan and starts animating it. That is the feedback loop that keeps a level populated after the spawn waves are spent." },
        { "id": "cmt_m1c62w", "address": "$86D7", "text": "ANIMATE DROIDS - and it is a screen scan, not an object loop. Every $05 of zapperFireCountdown it walks scanPtr from $0450 to $07FF, one byte at a time, and any character it finds in droidAnimFrameTable it advances to the next frame. A droid on the last frame ($12) calls DroidDropsBomb.\n\nSo the animation state of a droid is not in any array: it IS the character code sitting in screen memory. Damage works the same way - LaserHitsChar walks the same table backwards. The screen is the model, and the arrays hold only what the screen cannot: position history for the chain and the flag bits.\n\nTwo details worth knowing. The scan starts at $0450, which is row 2 column 0 - the top of the grid, deliberately below the status line. It ends when the pointer's high byte reaches $08, so it runs to $07FF, which is 24 bytes PAST the end of the 1000-byte screen at $07E7. Those bytes are the sprite pointer area; nothing writes them and nothing there matches the table, so the overrun is harmless, but it is an overrun." },
        { "id": "cmt_xggrtm", "address": "$8728", "text": "A pod has reached the last animation frame: turn it into a falling bomb. Write glyph $0A over the pod's cell, then scan bombPtrHi from slot 24 down for the $FF free marker and store the pod's screen pointer there - the bomb inherits the pod's position directly, with no coordinate conversion, because both are just a screen address.\n\nIf no slot is free the cell is written back as $12 instead, so the pod stays at the last frame and the next scan tries again. The cap is 24 bombs in flight and the failure mode is a delay, not a lost bomb." },
        { "id": "cmt_kc9aml", "address": "$8753", "text": "UPDATE BOMBS, every $40 iterations. Walks the 24 slots and steps each live one. The slots hold a raw screen pointer split across bombPtrLo/bombPtrHi rather than a column and a row, so moving down a row is just adding 40 - see MoveOneBomb." },
        { "id": "cmt_0sktzc", "address": "$876C", "text": "Move one bomb down one row. Reads the cell it is in (if that is the ship glyph $07, the player is dead), erases it, adds $28 to the pointer, then reads the destination: $20 means it has fallen off the bottom of the playfield and the slot is freed with $FF; $07 is again death; anything else and it draws itself in white.\n\nNote it does NOT go through PlotCharAndColour: it computes the colour address itself by adding $D4 to the pointer's high byte, the same trick, because it already holds a pointer and has no column/row to give the plot routine." },
        { "id": "cmt_6edcoq", "address": "$87CB", "text": "THE LASER HIT THIS CHARACTER. Search droidAnimFrameTable for it. Not found, so it is not a droid: LaserHitOtherChar. Found at index n: step BACK one frame - so a hit damages a droid rather than killing it, and a droid takes as many hits as its frame number. Index 1 is the last one: the cell is erased, ScoreDroidKill adds 10, and the shot is retired.\n\nBoth exits do PLA PLA before returning. This routine discards its own return address and returns to its caller's caller, skipping the rest of UpdateLaser - which is how a hit ends the laser's turn without a flag. It is the reason a naive call graph is wrong about UpdateLaser: the code after the JSR at $8535 is not reached through that call." },
        { "id": "cmt_kgperu", "address": "$8870", "text": "Add to the score. X is the digit position within scoreDigits (0 = most significant, 6 = least) and Y is how many times to increment it. Digits are the character codes $30-$39 directly in screen memory - there is no binary score anywhere - and a carry is `wrapped to $30 and DEX`. The score is literally the thing on the screen." },
        { "id": "cmt_3bk1hj", "address": "$88C9", "text": "UPDATE DROIDS. Runs once per $80 loop iterations, the same rate as the player.\n\nThree parallel arrays indexed 1..droidCount, all addressed as table-1,X: droidCol $1100, droidRow $1200, droidFlags $1300.\n\ndroidFlags is two markers and a direction, and getting these right is what explains the game:\n  bit6 ($40) - HEAD of a chain. Only a head is steered; everyone else follows.\n  bit7 ($80) - TAIL of a chain. Only a tail is erased.\n  bits 0/1   - the head's direction, flipped with EOR #$03 at $8962.\n\nThe chain runs from low index (head) to high index (tail), because SpawnDroids appends. A follower at index X simply copies the position of index X-1, one place ahead of it. So the update per step is: erase the tail cell, copy every position along by one, move the head. That is a snake, done in four instructions, and it is why there is no per-droid velocity anywhere.\n\nA head that reaches row $16 - below the grid - is put back to row $0E and has its direction bits forced, which is the drop-a-row-and-reverse sweep." },
        { "id": "cmt_jm0gk4", "address": "$89C1", "text": "SPAWN, one droid per call. A wave is a head followed by droidsPerWave-1 followers, all created at column $0A row $02 - the top of the grid - and appended to the arrays.\n\n  waveRemaining != 0 : append a follower with flags $00. The one that takes waveRemaining to 1 gets flags $80 (tail), and wavesLeft is decremented - a wave counts as spent when it is fully spawned, not when it is destroyed.\n  waveRemaining == 0 : count spawnCountdown down from $20 and, if wavesLeft is not 0, start the next wave with a head, flags $41 = bit6 plus direction 1.\n\nBoth wavesLeft and droidsPerWave come out of levelParameterTables, so the level number controls how many chains there are and how long each one is. Nothing else about a level changes except the zapper interval." },
        { "id": "cmt_ujthr0", "address": "$8AC8", "text": "Wave cleared: no waves left AND no droids left means the level is over, so jump to NewLevel. This is the only way to advance a level other than the title-screen select." },
        { "id": "cmt_658z0w", "address": "$8ADE", "text": "PLAYER DIES. Five callers, from all over the program and all at different call depths - the beam, a bomb, a droid, a collision. LDX #$F6 / TXS throws the whole call stack away rather than unwinding it, which is the only way to get out of, say, MoveOneBomb three frames deep inside UpdateBombs. That is why this is a jump target and not a subroutine, and why block analysis reports an undefined stack delta here." },
        { "id": "cmt_reibk0", "address": "$8AF8", "text": "PLAYER DEATH. All three voices are gated off, V1FREQHI is set to $03 and\nfrom then on only voice 1 is used, retriggered as noise every frame at\n$8B24 while SIGVOL is set to blastFrames - so the blast fades out as its\nanimation counts down. Eight fragments are seeded at the players cell and\nblown outward. Char $16 is the blast glyph, drawn in ORANGE." },
        { "id": "cmt_wyjpil", "address": "$8BEC", "text": "The player is standing on something. $08 and $09 are laser glyphs and $00 is an empty cell, so those are survivable; anything else is fatal. Note what this implies: walking into your own shot is harmless, which is not obvious from the drawing code." },
        { "id": "cmt_lydnmi", "address": "$8C17", "text": "LOSE A LIFE. Clears the playfield, decrements the lives digit - which is a character on the screen at $0427, there is no lives variable - and if it has reached '0' goes to CheckHighScore, i.e. game over. Otherwise RestartSameLevel." },
        { "id": "cmt_v0ghrd", "address": "$8C2D", "text": "NEW LEVEL. Resets the stack (this is reached by jump, not call), clears the playfield, prints \"BATTLE STATIONS\" and \"ENTER GRID AREA nn\", awards a life (the digit at $0427, capped at '9'), advances the level (capped at $20), and reads the three level parameters out of levelParameterTables. Then the fanfare, then StartLevel." },
        { "id": "cmt_67q7bj", "address": "$8D5E", "text": "Restart the level just lost. It decrements the lives digit and the level number and then falls into NewLevel, which increments both again - so the two DECs exist purely to cancel NewLevel's INCs. Replaying a level neither advances you nor gives you the free life." },
        { "id": "cmt_0gszzu", "address": "$81A2", "text": "Draw the grid, as an animation rather than a fill. First pass: for each column 2..$26 draw a bar ($3F) down rows 2..$15, with a note of the SID sweeping as the column advances. Second pass: for each row 2..$15 overwrite columns 1..$26 with the empty cell $00, again with sound. So the grid is drawn and then wiped to the playing field, and what you actually see is a sweep left-to-right and then top-to-bottom.\n\nNote $81D7/$81D8 and $820C/$820D: DEY / BNE to the very next instruction. The branch cannot go anywhere useful whichever way it falls - it is a two-byte no-op that only wastes time, i.e. hand-tuned delay." },
        { "id": "cmt_xnhb68", "address": "$8230", "text": "Inter-column delay for the grid wipe, whose whole job is a side effect.\nfxCounter is set to 4 and then INCremented until it wraps back to 4 -\n251 iterations - and every iteration writes V1FREQHI = gridCol and\nV2FREQHI = gridCol+1 at $8240. So the delay and the pitch sweep are the\nsame loop: the noise gets higher as the wipe moves right." },
        { "id": "cmt_iqdavt", "address": "$824F", "text": "THE MATERIALISE EFFECT, played once per level after the grid is drawn.\nAn expanding shape of blast glyphs around the players start cell\n(column $14, row $15), in WHITE, with fxCounter counting $0F down to $FF.\nEach step plots five cells - left, right, up-right, up and up-left of the\ncentre at distance fxCounter - so what you see is an X opening outwards,\nand the previous step is erased back to the grid glyph at $82B8. The glyph\nitself animates $16 -> $17 -> $18 and wraps, so the sparks flicker as they\ntravel. Note the CLC/SBC quirk documented at $8278: the leftward arms are\none column further out than the arithmetic reads.\nSound: voice 2 is retriggered as noise every step, and $8414 sets\nSIGVOL = VOLUME_MAX - fxCounter, so it grows LOUDER as it grows bigger -\nthe opposite of the death explosion, which fades.\nIt finishes by planting the ship glyph in LIGHT_GREEN at the centre and\nplaying three SweepDownSfx chirps at falling volume ($0F, $08, $02),\nwhich is the only triangle-wave sound in the game." },
        { "id": "cmt_go0df8", "address": "$8450", "text": "A falling chirp on voice 2, and the one place in the game that uses a\nTONE rather than noise: CTRL = $11 = triangle + gate. V2FREQHI counts\ndown from $18 to 1, so the pitch falls. Called three times in a row at\ndecreasing SIGVOL, which is the sound of the player materialising." },
        { "id": "cmt_cve0hg", "address": "$8573", "text": "The player laser sound. laserSfx counts down and is written to BOTH\nV1FREQHI and V2FREQHI, and both voices are retriggered as noise ($81)\nevery frame - so it is two noise generators at slightly different\nsettings sweeping down together, which is what gives the shot its\nragged zap rather than a clean tone. Silent when laserSfx is 0." },
        { "id": "cmt_aqil7m", "address": "$862F", "text": "Arm the zapper-beam sound: V3FREQHI = $03, about as low as the coarse\nhigh-byte-only pitch control goes. The caller then sets V3CTRL = $21,\nsawtooth + gate, so the beam is a low buzzing saw - the only sustained\npitched sound in the game." },
        { "id": "cmt_vvw7tg", "address": "$889A", "text": "The explosion sweep, on voice 3, one step every other tick.\nsfxFreq starts at $F0 and is DECremented into V3FREQHI while its top two\nbits are still set - i.e. down to $3F - with V3CTRL retriggered to $21\n(sawtooth) each step. That is a falling saw. When it bottoms out,\nsfxRepeats decides whether to reload $F0 and sweep again, so a bigger kill\nsounds like several descending swoops. With nothing left to do it parks\nV3FREQHI at $04 rather than gating off, leaving a low idle drone." },
        { "id": "cmt_bszub1", "address": "$8D16", "text": "Level-start fanfare: a descending two-tone sweep, $30 steps, while the two level digits at $055E/$055F flash by writing fanfareCounter straight into colour RAM at $D95E/$D95F. Falls through into StartLevel." },
        { "id": "cmt_liawtz", "address": "$8060", "placement": "inline", "text": "entered with X=0; HighScoreNextDigit re-enters the loop with X=1..6" },
        { "id": "cmt_myveyk", "address": "$8172", "placement": "after", "text": "Verified by execution: with screenPtr=$0400, Y=$0A, plotChar=$07, plotColour=$0D this block writes $07 to $040A and $0D to $D80A, and leaves screenPtr pointing at $D800. Every caller must go through ScreenPtrForPlotRow again - the pointer is destroyed by the call." },
        { "id": "cmt_19xu56", "address": "$8906", "text": "A follower: copy the position of the droid one index ahead - droidCol[X] = droidCol[X-1], droidRow[X] = droidRow[X-1] - and draw it as char $13. This is the entire follow behaviour, and it is why the array order IS the chain order and why removing a droid has to compact rather than leave a hole." },
        { "id": "cmt_ot0frt", "address": "$892A", "text": "The leader. Direction is droidFlags bits 0/1; the step is written as INC INC DEC or INC DEC so the sign comes out of how many INCs are skipped, the same trick the death-explosion deltas use. Hitting row $16 (below the grid) wraps to row $0E and flips the direction bits." },
        { "id": "cmt_20ff99", "address": "$8A53", "text": "Compact the droid arrays: shift every entry from X upward down by one and decrement droidCount. This is why droid identity is an array index and nothing else - there is no id, and a droid destroyed in the middle makes every droid above it change index. PromoteNewSquadLeader runs first so the leader bit is ORed into the survivor before the shuffle moves it." },
        { "id": "cmt_102jqx", "address": "$86D0", "text": "Advance the beam character and - the part that matters - decrement `phase` a second time. FireZapperBeam only runs when phase is exactly 5, so without this the beam would get one iteration per 24; with it, the beam holds the slot by pushing phase along itself." },
        { "id": "cmt_of6jpw", "address": "$8806", "text": "Draw the 40-character status line and its colours from statusLineCharTable / statusLineColourTable. The loop runs X = $28 down to 1 over `table-1`, so its last fetch is one byte PAST the end of the character table - which is $8847, the first byte of the colour table, $34 = the character 4. That is where the initial four lives comes from: it is the first colour byte, read as a character, because the two tables are adjacent. ResetScoreAndLives $80E5 writes the same $34 explicitly, so whether the overrun is deliberate or a coincidence that happens to work cannot be settled from the code alone." },
        { "id": "cmt_d906ck", "address": "$8870", "placement": "inline", "text": "X = digit column, 0 = millions, 6 = units; Y = how many increments" },
        { "id": "cmt_36lbva", "address": "$888A", "placement": "inline", "text": "+10: ten increments of the units digit" },
        { "id": "cmt_u5sp2h", "address": "$89A0", "text": "tick has wrapped past 0 to $FF; put it back to $80. This is the only thing that ever writes tick apart from UpdatePlayer's DEC, and it is what makes the player's movement period 128 loop iterations rather than 256." },
        { "id": "cmt_yuru7l", "address": "$8A11", "text": "The laser hit something that is not in droidAnimFrameTable, so not a droid in its damage animation. Three characters are worth points and everything else is ignored - the shot passes straight through a zapper glyph or another bomb.\n\n  $13 -> +100   (a chain follower; every non-leader droid draws itself as $13)\n  $14 -> +400   (a leader; droidChar cycles $13,$14,$15 at $88DA)\n  $15 -> +400\n\nThe arithmetic: AddToScore takes X = digit position within scoreDigits, 0 being the most significant of seven, and Y = how many times to increment it. X=4 is the hundreds column, so Y=3 at $8A21 is +300 and it then falls through into $8A28 for another +100. Compare ScoreDroidKill, which uses X=6 Y=$0A - ten increments of the units column, i.e. +10 for finishing off a damaged droid.\n\nThis routine also discards its own return address at $8A33 (PLA PLA), like LaserHitsChar, and then walks the droid array at $8A37 to find and remove whatever it just shot." },
        { "id": "cmt_rnyqtv", "address": "$8A21", "placement": "inline", "text": "+300, then falls through for +100 more" },
        { "id": "cmt_1sa7fh", "address": "$8A99", "text": "Score and make the explosion noise. Sets the sfxFreq sweep that ExplosionSfxStep plays out over the following iterations, then tail-calls AddToScore with whatever X and Y the caller set." },
        { "id": "cmt_bgtb3d", "address": "$8995", "text": "Plot only if droidFlags bit7 is set, i.e. only if this droid is the TAIL of its chain. The caller has already loaded the erase character, so this is the tail-erase half of the snake update. A droid in the middle is never erased because the droid behind it is about to draw over its cell anyway." },
        { "id": "cmt_6xf8qq", "address": "$8A49", "text": "Which repair is needed depends on what the destroyed droid was:\n  neither bit ($00) - a middle droid: PromoteNewSquadLeader splits the chain\n  both bits ($C0)   - a chain of one: nothing to repair\n  bit6 only ($40)   - the head: OR its flags into X+1, which inherits the lead and the direction\n  bit7 only ($80)   - the tail: OR $80 into X-1, which becomes the new tail\nOnly then is the array compacted." },
        { "id": "cmt_fpkkbt", "address": "$8AA4", "text": "A droid in the MIDDLE of a chain was destroyed - neither head (bit6) nor tail (bit7). Splitting the chain in two is what happens next, and it is the most interesting thing in the program.\n\nScan down from X until a droid with bit6 is found: that is the head of this chain, and its flags carry the direction. OR those flags into index X+1, so the droid behind the gap becomes a head in its own right, travelling the same way. Then OR $80 into index X-1, so the droid in front of the gap becomes a tail.\n\nOne chain in, two chains out, each with its own head and tail, sharing one array. Shooting the middle of a squad does not shorten it - it doubles the number of things chasing you." },
        { "id": "cmt_satobt", "address": "$8100", "placement": "after", "text": "Verified: constant folding through the $02/$03 pointer resolves all three writes to $D018 = $18, $D020 = $00, $D021 = $00. They are the only VIC register writes in the program. $D015 (sprite enable), $D01A (interrupt enable) and $0314 (IRQ vector) are never written anywhere - searched as byte patterns, not just as decoded instructions." },
        { "id": "cmt_1a6yfx", "address": "$8906", "placement": "after", "text": "Verified by execution: with X=3 and droidCol[2]=$11, droidRow[2]=$09, this block writes $11 and $09 into droidCol[3] and droidRow[3] and sets plotChar to $13 - element 3 takes element 2's position. That is the follow, proved rather than read off." },
        { "id": "cmt_mpagyv", "address": "$8ADE", "placement": "after", "text": "Seven inbound references, five of them live: CharIsPlayerThenDie, MoveOneBomb (twice), BombPosOrDeath and DieUnlessHarmlessChar. The other two are in the unreachable fragments at $8AD4 and $8AF1." },
        { "id": "cmt_xsv9w1", "address": "$8CB5", "text": "Three parallel tables read at $8C8C with X = level, one-based. The bases $8CB4, $8CD4 and $8CF4 are never read as entries themselves - $8CB4 is the high byte of the JMP at $8CB2, and $8CD4/$8CF4 are the next table's base - so nothing is wasted by indexing from 1.\n\nThe curve, level: waves x droids-per-wave, zapper fire interval:\n\n  1: 1x6/16   2: 2x6/16   3: 2x6/16   4: 3x7/15   5: 3x7/14   6: 3x8/13\n  7: 4x8/12   8: 4x9/11   9: 4x12/10 10: 4x12/9  11: 5x10/9  12: 5x10/9\n 13: 16x3/9  14: 6x15/9  15: 6x16/9  16: 6x16/9  17: 6x17/8  18: 6x18/8\n 19: 6x19/8  20: 6x20/8  21: 6x20/7  22: 6x20/7  23: 6x21/7  24: 6x21/7\n 25: 6x22/7  26: 6x22/7  27: 7x22/7  28: 7x23/7  29: 7x3/7   30: 7x24/6\n 31: 7x24/6\n\nThirty-one levels, not thirty-two. NewLevel increments `level` and, if it has reached $20, decrements it straight back, so `level` can never exceed $1F. The tables are 32 bytes apart and indexed from 1, so entry $20 of each - the bytes at $8CD4, $8CF4 and $8D14 - can never be read. $8D14 = $05 is the only one of the three that is not doing a second job as another table's base, so it is one byte of genuinely dead data: the parameters for a level 32 that the cap makes unreachable.\n\nTwo entries are outliers and the code cannot say which they are. Level 13 is 16 waves of 3 where its neighbours are 5-6 waves of 10-15; BOTH of its numbers jump at once, which is what a transposed pair looks like, but 48 short chains is also a perfectly good boss level. Level 29 is 7 waves of 3 between neighbours of 23 and 24 droids, which reads as a breather two levels from the end. What would settle it: play 13 and 29 in an emulator, or diff these 96 bytes against a second dump of the cartridge.\n\nPast level 20 the curve is nearly flat - the interval bottoms out at 6 and the droid count creeps by one every other level - so the game stops getting harder rather than getting hard. Level 31 then repeats for ever." },
        { "id": "cmt_hkp57y", "address": "$8806", "placement": "inline", "text": "only caller is BootDrawStatusLine, so the high score survives a game" },
        { "id": "cmt_w17ogc", "address": "$8C75", "placement": "inline", "text": "a free life per level, capped at 9" },
        { "id": "cmt_sdnj7d", "address": "$8C82", "placement": "inline", "text": "cap: $20 is pushed back to $1F, so 31 is the last level" },
        { "id": "cmt_7q8ngp", "address": "$8D14", "text": "Unreachable. This is entry $20 of levelZapperInterval, and `level` is capped at $1F, so nothing ever indexes here. The corresponding entries of the other two tables ($8CD4, $8CF4) are unreachable for the same reason but are doing double duty as the next table's base; this byte is not." },
        { "id": "cmt_hi8uw2", "address": "$8E58", "text": "Glyphs $0B and $0C, and they are DEAD ART. They are exact horizontal\nmirrors of one another (00 C0 72 1F 1F 72 C0 00 against\n00 03 4E F8 F8 4E 03 00 - every byte bit-reversed), a left- and\nright-facing pair. Nothing draws them: find_immediates says neither $0B\nnor $0C is ever loaded anywhere in the cartridge, they are not in\ndroidAnimFrames, and they appear in none of the five character tables\n($8080, $8820, $8C51, $8DE1, $871F). Every other glyph is reachable.\nMy guess is a discarded pair of horizontal enemies; that is a guess, and\nwhat would settle it is an earlier build, not this binary." },
        { "id": "cmt_h7rny7", "address": "$8ED8", "text": "Glyphs $1B and $1C are the letter Y, and they are BYTE-IDENTICAL:\nboth C6 C6 00 7C 38 38 38 38. Only $1B is used - \"BY JEFF MINTER\" at\n$8DE1 begins 29 1B. $1C is a duplicate nothing refers to.\nWorth noticing because it is the only letter in the font that appears\ntwice, which is what you would expect if the alphabet was packed by hand\nin the order it was needed and one letter got added twice." },
        { "id": "cmt_qya6ot", "address": "$8EF8", "text": "Glyph $1F, an up arrow, drawn by nothing. Like $0B/$0C it is never loaded\nand appears in no character table. Together with $0B, $0C and the\nduplicate $1C, four of the 64 glyphs are unreachable - about 6% of the\nfont is left-over or spare art." },
        { "id": "cmt_xeummw", "address": "$8070", "text": "The first digit that differs decides it. CMP has already run, so N is set if the score digit is LOWER than the high-score digit - BMI means no record. Falling through means higher, so copy all seven digits across. Reaching the end of the loop without a difference means equal, which also counts as no record." },
        { "id": "cmt_lvszj6", "address": "$8380", "text": "Delay, but only if fxCounter is non-zero - so the sound effects that use fxCounter as a radius get shorter as the effect converges, without the caller having to say so." },
        { "id": "cmt_qosuvh", "address": "$8386", "text": "Busy-wait. Two nested loops of DEC delayCounter / BNE from 0, so 256 x 256 iterations - about a third of a second. This and the DEX/BNE at MainLoopDelay are the only timing in the program; there is no raster wait anywhere." },
        { "id": "cmt_31s4hk", "address": "$8A37", "text": "The laser hit a droid, but the character on the screen does not say WHICH array entry it was - the arrays are the only place a droid's identity lives. So walk droidCol/droidRow from droidCount down looking for the entry whose position matches, and that is the one to remove. Linear, and it runs once per hit, so at most 25 comparisons." },
        { "id": "cmt_z5wjrf", "address": "$8B24", "text": "One frame of the death explosion, repeated 15 times (blastFrames). Each frame: erase every fragment cell that still holds the PREVIOUS blast character - the CMP blastChar at $8B4C is what stops the explosion scribbling over droids or the grid - then advance blastChar ($16,$17,$18 cycling), move all eight fragments outward by their delta vectors, and draw them. A fragment that walks off the grid is reset to the player's last position by StepFragCol/StepFragRow, so the burst folds back on itself rather than escaping." },
        { "id": "cmt_2zhn4n", "address": "$8BFC", "text": "Clear the playfield: fill $0450-$07FF with $20. Note this leaves row 0 ($0400-$0427) alone, which is why the score, the high score and the lives digit survive it - they ARE row 0.\n\nIt uses $07/$08 as its pointer, which are screenPtr+1 and gridCol everywhere else in the program. Nothing is corrupted because it is only called between frames, but the second reading is real and is bound here as clearPtrLo/clearPtrHi.\n\nLike AnimateDroidsOnScreen it stops when the high byte reaches $08, so it writes 24 bytes past the end of the 1000-byte screen, into the sprite pointer area. Harmless in a game with no sprites." },
        { "id": "cmt_fql25y", "address": "$8C35", "text": "Print the two level banners: \"BATTLE STATIONS\" at $04FE in light blue and \"ENTER GRID AREA 00\" at $054E in white. Both tables are indexed one-based from a base that is the last byte of the preceding instruction, the same byte-saving trick the level tables use." },
        { "id": "cmt_k1kjvp", "address": "$821B", "text": "Pause on P. $C5 is the KERNAL's current-key-pressed byte and $29 is the matrix code for P; the game never scans the keyboard itself, it reads what the KERNAL IRQ has already put there.\n\nThe sequence is deliberately four-stage and reads oddly: return if P is not down; wait for it to be released; wait for it to be pressed again; then WaitKeyPRelease. So the first press pauses and holds, and the second press resumes. Both edges are waited for, which is what stops one long press toggling the pause dozens of times.\n\nNote $821F BNE goes to the RTS at $821A, which is UpdateZappers' RTS. Two routines sharing an RTS to save a byte." },
        { "id": "cmt_7g6rpt", "address": "$8373", "placement": "after", "text": "These two instructions - here and the identical one at $8DB0 - are the ONLY reads of anything in the I/O page in the entire program. Verified with find_instructions over $D000-$DFFF for LDA: two hits, both this. No raster register is read, no SID oscillator, no CIA timer, no jiffy clock.\n\nThe consequence is worth stating plainly: Gridrunner has no source of randomness. Droid spawn positions are fixed at column $0A row $02, wave sizes come out of a table, the zappers sweep on a fixed period, and the beams fire on a per-level interval. Given the same starting level and the same joystick inputs, the game plays out identically every time." },
        { "id": "cmt_r94igd", "address": "$83D4", "text": "CLI, and this needs saying because it is easy to conclude the opposite. The game installs no interrupt handler of its own - $0314 is never written and $D01A is never touched - but it does NOT run with interrupts off. JSR ROM_RESTOR at $83CE puts the KERNAL's default vectors back and IOINIT has already enabled CIA1 timer A, so from this instruction on the ordinary 60Hz KERNAL IRQ is running underneath the whole game.\n\nThat matters for two reasons. It is what keeps $C5 (the currently-pressed key) updated, which is the only reason PauseOnKeyP works at all - nothing in this program scans the keyboard. And it means the game is not free of interrupts, it is merely not SYNCHRONISED to them: the main loop never waits for anything and the KERNAL IRQ steals time from it at unpredictable points." },
        { "id": "cmt_jatw42", "address": "$882C", "text": "$19 $1A - the two-cell label in front of the SCORE field, and the one thing\non this screen I could not read.\n\nEvidence for what it labels: scoreDigits is $040F-$0415 (columns 15-21) and\nhiScoreDigits is $041B-$0421 (columns 27-33), confirmed by AddToScore $8870\nand CheckHighScore $8055. These two cells sit at columns 12-13, one blank\nbefore the score. The matching pair before the hi-score field is $1D $1E,\nwhich is unambiguously H, I and a colon - so this is the same kind of thing,\nin the same small 5-pixel-tall style and the same YELLOW ($07).\n\nThe pixels, both cells side by side:\n  ................\n  ####..##......##\n  ##.##.##......##\n  ####..##......##\n  ##....##......##\n  ##....####.##.##\nCompare $1D $1E, which parses cleanly as H(6px) I(4px) colon(3px):\n  ##..##..####....\n  ##..##...##..##.\n  ######...##.....\n  ##..##...##..##.\n  ##..##..####....\nOn the same grammar this reads P(6px) L(6px) and then a 4px stroke, which\nspells nothing. Note the font has NO letter C, so \"SC\" could not have been\nset in the alphabet even if that is what it means - which is a reason to\nsuspect these two cells are a hand-drawn pictogram rather than letters.\nWhat would settle it: one screenshot of the running cartridge. Nothing in\nthe bytes will." },
        { "id": "cmt_yndwgh", "address": "$817C", "placement": "inline", "text": "$04xx screen -> $D8xx colour RAM. Verified by running this block: with screenPtr=$0640, Y=$14, plotChar=$07, plotColour=$0D it wrote $07 to $0654 and $0D to $DA54." },
        { "id": "cmt_trk5j5", "address": "$83E8", "placement": "after", "text": "NOTHING IN THIS GAME IS SYNCHRONISED TO THE RASTER. $D011 and $D012 are\nnever read or written, the IRQ vector at $0314 is never touched, and the\nonly interrupt entry is the NMI stub at $83E2 that does nothing. Every\ncharacter is poked straight into $0400 whenever the game loop gets round to\nit, so the picture tears by design and the frame rate is whatever the code\nhappens to take. That is also why the sound has to be driven inline, one\nregister write at a time, from the same loop that draws." },
        { "id": "cmt_3ru4nj", "address": "$8278", "text": "CLC before SBC, and the program does this EVERYWHERE. There are six real `CLC / SBC` sites - $8278, $828C, $82C9, $82E4, $8411 and $869A - and not one `SEC / SBC` anywhere in the 4K. On a 6502 SBC subtracts the carry's complement, so a cleared carry takes an extra 1: every subtraction in this program is one less than it reads.\n\nProved rather than argued: running the block at $8264 with fxCounter = $0F leaves plotCol = $04. $14 - $0F is $05. The effect is one column further left than the arithmetic says.\n\nAll six sites are inside visual effects - the materialise diamond, the volume ramp, the beam glyph - where being one out is invisible, which is presumably why it survived. It is worth knowing before anyone tries to reproduce the geometry of the level-start effect and finds it will not line up." },
        { "id": "cmt_l043um", "address": "$869A", "placement": "inline", "text": "CLC before SBC: this is beamChar - 2" },
        { "id": "cmt_dgju37", "address": "$86C5", "placement": "inline", "text": "a new pod at the beam intersection" },
        { "id": "cmt_qbarsn", "address": "$871F", "text": "THE POD LIFE CYCLE, which is the game's central mechanic and is entirely encoded in this nine-byte table.\n\nThere are two enemy types and they are represented completely differently:\n\n  CHAIN DROIDS - glyphs $13 (segment) and $14/$15 (head). They live in the droidCol/droidRow/droidFlags arrays, move as snakes, are created by SpawnDroids from the level tables, and die in one laser hit.\n\n  PODS - glyphs $18, $0D, $0E, $0F, $10, $11, $12, in that order, which is this table read from index 1 to 7. A pod is held NOWHERE except as a character on the screen: no array, no coordinates, no id. AnimateDroidsOnScreen finds it by scanning the whole playfield and advances it one frame.\n\nA pod ages forward $18 -> $0D -> ... -> $12, and at $12 DroidDropsBomb turns the cell into a bomb ($0A) and hands the screen address to a bomb slot. A laser hit steps it BACKWARD, one frame per hit, and destroying it at $18 scores 10.\n\nWhere pods come from is the part worth knowing. Glyph $0F - four frames into the sequence, so four hits from death - is written by exactly two instructions in the program:\n\n  $86C5, in EraseZapperBeam: at the point where the two zapper beams cross.\n  $8A71, in CompactDroidArrays: at the cell of a chain droid you just shot.\n\nSo killing a chain segment does not remove it, it leaves a pod behind that will grow and bomb you; and the zappers manufacture pods every time they fire. Both routes make more work than they remove, which is why the level parameter that rises fastest is how often the zappers fire.\n\nIndex 0 ($EA) and index 8 ($13) are never read by any of the three loops over this table. $13 being just past the end, and being the chain-segment glyph, is a coincidence worth noticing and not reading anything into: LaserHitOtherChar handles $13 with its own CMP." },
        { "id": "cmt_rgjhov", "address": "$8A6D", "text": "The droid is gone from the arrays - now leave a POD in its place. Glyph $0F in yellow at plotCol/plotRow, which still hold the position FindDroidAtPlotPos matched. $0F is four frames into droidAnimFrameTable, so the pod needs four more laser hits and will drop a bomb if you leave it.\n\nThis is why shooting a chain is not obviously progress, and it is the difference between this game and a shooting gallery." },
        { "id": "cmt_ajdi92", "address": "$8A71", "placement": "inline", "text": "a new pod, four frames from death, where the droid was" }
      ],
